.TH RADIOSTYX 1
.SH NAME
radiostyx \- Styx over the radio, and the message size it settles on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiostyx
.B serve
.RB [ lora ]
.RB [ ssl ]
.RB [ auth ]
.br
.B radiostyx
.B get
.RI [ path
.RB [ lora ]
.RB [ ssl ]
.RB [ auth ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Serve
exports the namespace over the radio.
.B Get
walks to
.I path
on the other end, reads it and prints it, first printing the message
size the two agreed on.  With
.B lora
both go through
.IR lora (3)
\- addressed, and able to acknowledge \- rather than straight at the
packet device; with
.B ssl
both push
.IR ssl (3)
on top of whichever that was; with
.B auth
the key
.I ssl (3)
is given is the one
.IR keyring (2)'s
.B auth
has just derived over that same link, and each end has proved to the
other which certified identity it holds.
.PP
It exists to run the argument in
.B doc/pycubed-plan.ms
that a satellite needs no change to speak Styx over a radio-sized
message, because
.B exportfs
adopts the transport's
.B iounit
when negotiating \-
.B os/inferno/port/exportfs.c:806
turns an iounit above 64 and below the requested size into the size.
.PP
The ground half does not use
.IR mount (2),
on purpose.
.B os/inferno/port/devmnt.c:195
refuses a message size below 256 and a radio payload is 255 at most,
which is the argument's whole point: the floor is on the client, and
the client is a workstation.
.B appl/lib/styx.b
has no floor, so this speaks Styx directly.
.SH EXAMPLE
On the satellite,
.B "radiostyx serve" ;
on the ground,
.BR "radiostyx get dev/battery" .
The ground asks for 8192 and prints what it got:
.IP
.EX
RADIOSTYX-PAYLOAD 255
RADIOSTYX-MSIZE 255 version 9P2000
RADIOSTYX-READ '3960
'
.EE
.PP
255 is the radio's payload budget, arrived at by the server without
being told.  Four measurements, two stations, both
.BR mps2-an386 :
.IP
.EX
	plain              255
	lora               251
	ssl                225
	lora ssl           221
	lora auth          221
.EE
.PP
Every step of that is a subtraction somebody has to do and publish.
.IR Lora (3)
spends four bytes on the RadioHead header and republishes the rest;
.IR ssl (3)
spends thirty on its record header, digest and padding and republishes
the rest; and
.B exportfs
adopts whatever it is handed.  Nothing in the chain is told there is a
radio at the bottom of it.
.PP
With
.BR auth ,
the same two stations print
.PP
.EX
	RADIOSTYX-LORA #L/0 addr 1
	RADIOSTYX-PAYLOAD 251
	RADIOSTYX-AUTH peer satellite secret 128
	RADIOSTYX-SSL #D/0
	RADIOSTYX-MSIZE 221 version 9P2000
	RADIOSTYX-READ '3960
	'
.EE
.PP
and the satellite prints
.B "peer ground"
for the same exchange.  That is the whole of what
.B doc/pycubed-plan.ms
argues for: a file read off a satellite over a link that is
authenticated and encrypted, with no IP stack anywhere in it, and a
message size neither end was told.
.PP
.B "\(dqEncrypted\(dq here is SHA-1 and RC4-256, and that is worth stating plainly."
It is what
.IR ssl (3)
offers \-
.B encrypttab
in
.B /os/inferno/port/devssl.c
has RC4 at 40, 128 and 256 bits, DES at 40 and 56, and IDEA \- and
.B "RC4 is not a modern cipher."
This makes the bytes unreadable to somebody listening on 915MHz with a
software radio, which is the threat
.B doc/radio-plan.ms
describes; it is not security against anybody who has read about RC4.
.PP
It asked for
.B "alg sha"
until October 2026, which
.IR ssl (3)
documents as a DIGEST: the bytes went out readable while this page, the
source and the plan all called the link encrypted.
.SH BUGS
The client is one exchange deep: one walk, one open, one read, no
clunk, and no second read for a file longer than one reply.  It is a
demonstration of the transport, not a file system client.
.PP
With
.B lora
the addresses are 1 for the ground and 2 for the satellite, both ends
connect, and acknowledgement and retry are on: the authentication
exchange is symmetric and its first transmissions collide, so without
retransmission it does not complete.  With
.B auth
the identity each end presents follows from which of the two it is
running as, so what the exchange proves is which side of this test the
other end is, and not which station.  Both are written into the
program; two stations is what the demonstration needs, and three would
need somewhere to say which is which.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/radiostyx.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR radio (3),
.IR lora (3),
.IR radiossl (1),
.IR styx (2)
